# The Adversarial Accuracy Loop — a reusable method

*A high-level, project-agnostic playbook distilled from the DW Bridges dictation-cleanup campaign
(2026-08). Use it to bootstrap any project where the goal is: maximize AI autonomy while improving
accuracy, without constant user back-and-forth — and without the AI fooling itself.*

---

## The core idea in one paragraph

Many AI agents **propose** in parallel; a piece of **code judges** them against measured reference
data; a **human rules only at decision gates**; and a **curated failure ledger** carries every
lesson forward so no agent ever re-walks a dead end. The user's time is spent on the ~5 decisions
that actually need human judgment, not on supervising the work. Everything else runs autonomously —
and the system is built so that being *wrong* gets caught by machinery, not by hoping someone
notices.

---

## What we actually did, step by step

### 1. Established ground truth FIRST (before any solution work)
- Built a **gold-standard reference set** (40 human-approved examples) defining what "correct"
  means. The user calibrated it: a 10-item sample review settled 5 explicit policy questions
  (what may be changed, what must never be), then the remaining 30 were authored to that standard.
- An **independent adversarial agent audited the gold set itself** before it froze (0 violations,
  6 minors fixed via visible errata).
- **Rule: outside datasets and model outputs are SIGNAL, never ground truth.** Only the
  user-calibrated gold set judges correctness.

### 2. Built the judge before the contestants
- A **fixed contract** every proposal must implement: one function signature, with **ABSTAIN as a
  first-class answer** ("I'm not sure" is always legal and always safe). This encodes the safety
  law in the API instead of trusting each author's discipline.
- An **automated harness** that scores every proposal against reference data in seconds — so
  testing 20 ideas costs the same as testing 1. That's what makes massive parallelism *useful*
  instead of just noisy.
- **The harness self-test**: before trusting any number, the judge must (a) give a perfect oracle a
  perfect score and (b) catch a known-bad rule. Ours FAILED its first self-test — which exposed two
  real defects that would have silently corrupted every later verdict.
- **A disqualifying metric with a hard bar**: "over-reach" (destroying content the reference kept)
  ranks above everything. A high-coverage rule that violates it is disqualified, never "tuned later."

### 3. Spread the thinking across many models — structured, not scattered
- **6 proposer agents** in one orchestrated workflow, forced onto **different angles** (so they
  don't produce six copies of the same idea) across **two tracks** (pure-code rules vs
  model-assisted rules), on **mixed model types** for genuine viewpoint diversity.
- **Hard requirement: proposals arrive as EXECUTABLE CODE against the contract.** Prose is an
  opinion, and plausible-sounding opinions are exactly what produced the original bug. Anything
  that can't be run through the harness is auto-rejected.
- **Pipelined, not batched**: the moment each proposal landed, a *different* agent attacked it —
  no waiting for all six.

### 4. Attacked everything that looked good (the part that mattered most)
- **Adversarial critics** whose only job is to break a rule: "produce an input where this destroys
  real content." Agreeableness = failure. Critics must **run code and quote real inputs/outputs**,
  not speculate.
- **Held-out validation**: the development data the authors could see is in-sample by definition.
  We scored everything again on a 3.4× larger unseen split. **This overturned the headline result**
  — the "perfect" broad-coverage winner (0.00% violations in-sample) crossed the disqualification
  bar on fresh data (0.93%), while the narrow evidence-demanding rule survived (0.18%).
- **Domain validation**: rules built on an outside corpus were re-tested on OUR OWN data before
  being believed. This caught a total transfer failure the corpus could never show (69% of trigger
  words that are disfluency markers in the corpus are ordinary words in real dictation).
- **The lead re-verifies critic claims before recording them.** One critic's claim was partially
  wrong; verification corrected it.

### 5. Maintained the shared memory (the anti-retesting document you asked about)
- **A RULE-ATTEMPTS LEDGER**: every attempt — success, failure, or discovery — recorded with its
  measured result and a **class-level lesson** ("left-scanning heuristics fail BECAUSE they are
  blind to the correction on the right"), not just the instance.
- Every new round of agents **reads the ledger first** and must propose net-new mechanisms or fix a
  recorded failure mode. Re-proposals of ledgered dead ends are auto-rejected.
- Three rules keep the ledger honest:
  1. **Curated from measured results only** — never from a model's own post-mortem (a wrong
     self-diagnosis would be inherited by every future round).
  2. **"X failed BECAUSE Y"**, never "don't touch X" — because sometimes the fix is a close cousin
     of the failure (our best mechanism was a cousin of the worst one).
  3. **Single curator.** Multiple writers corrupt shared memory.

### 6. Kept the human at the gates, not in the loop
The user ruled on: the goal + fidelity policy (5 calibration questions), go/no-go gates between
phases, scope changes, and final ship decisions. Roughly **five decisions** across a campaign that
ran hundreds of experiments. Everything else — proposing, testing, attacking, recording — ran
autonomously under standing law.

