## What worked (keep these)

| Practice | Why it earned its place |
|---|---|
| **Code judges, models propose** | Every result is reproducible; no debate about whether a rule "seems good" |
| **Executable proposals only** | Killed the plausible-but-wrong failure mode at the door |
| **ABSTAIN as first-class** | Rules compete on precision; safety is structural, not behavioral |
| **Harness self-test before use** | Caught 2 judge defects that would have invalidated everything downstream |
| **Held-out split** | Overturned the headline winner — the single highest-value check in the campaign |
| **Own-domain validation** | Caught a 100% transfer failure that corpus numbers could never reveal |
| **Adversarial critics with run-code obligation** | Found a fatal punctuation-dependent bypass, case-dependence, and a root cause the lead had missed |
| **The failure ledger** | Later agents demonstrably built on lessons instead of re-deriving them |
| **Forced angle diversity** | The 6 proposals were genuinely different mechanisms, not paraphrases |
| **Workflow orchestration (pipeline, not barrier)** | ~12 agents, critiques starting as proposals landed; survived a session-limit crash and resumed from cache |
| **Reading audits (human-readable spot checks) before any verdict** | Metrics lied repeatedly: a "perfect" score hid punctuation corruption; an inflated failure count was a tokenization artifact |
| **Splitting metrics by difficulty** | The old rule looked 2× better than it was because half the test cases were trivially easy — the split exposed it |

## What didn't work / traps we fell into (avoid these)

| Trap | What happened | The rule that prevents it |
|---|---|---|
| **In-sample optimism** | Two rules scored 0.00% violations on data their authors could read, then failed fresh data | Nothing is believed until scored on data no author has seen |
| **The oracle input** | Our test harness silently handed every rule a guaranteed-valid trigger; production has no such oracle | Ask of every harness: "what does production NOT know that this test provides?" |
| **Synthetic probes** | A latency probe on a short fixed input under-measured real cost by 3× | Measure with real inputs at real sizes before believing a budget |
| **Threshold tuning = curve fitting** | An author swept a cap until failures vanished and called it "load-bearing"; failures were simply monotone in the cap — he'd found the edge of the dataset, not the mechanism | Any threshold tuned until dev-set failures vanish is a fit to that set |
| **Corpus convenience** | The outside corpus made trigger-detection free by construction, hiding that it's the actual hard problem in our domain | List what the corpus gives you for free; assume each freebie is a landmine |
| **Metrics blind spots** | Case-insensitive and punctuation-stripping metrics hid real damage three separate times | Every metric must be paired with a reading audit; add a strict variant of any lossy metric |
| **Median over mixed populations** | "~1.2s median" hid that long inputs took 11.5s — the median was dominated by easy short cases | Always report per-population numbers when the population is bimodal |
| **Contract loopholes** | One agent returned "do nothing" instead of ABSTAIN, inflating its coverage stat | Validate contract compliance mechanically, not by trust |

