## The reusable checklist (start a new project here)

1. **Define correctness with the user**: build a small gold set; have the user calibrate it on a
   sample; freeze it; adversarially audit it. Write the non-negotiable law (our "never destroy
   content") explicitly.
2. **Build the judge**: contract with ABSTAIN + automated harness + disqualifying metric +
   **self-test the judge before trusting it**.
3. **Reserve held-out data now** — before any author sees anything. Also reserve a final
   certification set that is touched exactly once, at the very end.
4. **Seed the failure ledger** with anything already known.
5. **Fan out proposers** (workflow): forced angles, two tracks, executable-only, pipelined critics.
6. **Judge**: harness → held-out → own-domain → reading audit. Lead verifies critic claims.
7. **Curate the ledger** (single curator, measured results only, class-level lessons).
8. **Repeat until dry** (two rounds with no survivors), then bring the user the leaderboard AND the
   graveyard.
9. **User gates**: policy calibration, phase go/no-go, ship/shelve. Nothing else needs them.

---

## The honest bottom line from our run

The loop *worked* — but note what "worked" means: **its biggest wins were catching its own errors.**
Round 1 produced two rules that looked perfect and a composed winner; held-out testing disqualified
the winner; adversarial critique found a fatal bypass in the runner-up; domain validation blocked
even the survivor from shipping; and the convergent conclusion (three independent lines of evidence
pointing at the same upstream problem) redirected the whole effort at the *right* question. A naive
version of this campaign would have shipped a broken rule with a perfect-looking scorecard at every
one of those steps. The method's value is not that it produces winners faster — it's that it makes
being wrong *loud* while letting the human stay out of the room until a decision is genuinely theirs.
